---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import Command from '../../components/Command.astro'
import { connectors, doc, piDoc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['adapter', '1. Install the adapter'],
  ['connect', '2. Connect a service'],
  ['verify', '3. Test and use it'],
  ['catalog', 'Connector requirements'],
  ['custom', 'Custom MCP servers'],
  ['permissions', 'Permissions and troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Connect your services"
  description="Let the agent use the services you pick. Install the MCP adapter, sign in to a service, and test the connection before you rely on it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="adapter">
    <h2>1. Install the MCP adapter</h2>
    <p>
      MCP is the protocol. A connector is one configured MCP server. pi has MCP support of its own,
      but Phosphor’s connectors work through the <strong>MCP adapter</strong> package, which replaces
      it in sessions and handles sign-in. Install it from Settings → Extensions, or run:
    </p>
    <Command cmd="pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter" label="Install the MCP adapter" />
    <p>
      <strong>Settings → Extensions → MCP Connectors</strong> shows up before the adapter is installed,
      and its Advanced section can install it too. Installing the adapter doesn’t connect any service
      by itself.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="connect">
    <h2>2. Add and authorize a service</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>MCP Connectors</strong>.</li><li>
        Find the service under <strong>Add a connector</strong> and fill in any required fields, like
        the Datadog site or Slack client ID.
      </li><li>
        Choose <strong>Add</strong>. Phosphor writes the configuration and starts the adapter’s
        authorization flow.
      </li><li>
        Finish signing in through your browser. Check the scopes and which account or workspace
        you’re granting.
      </li><li>
        Go back to Phosphor. If the adapter asks for a callback URL, follow its prompt. Don’t share
        that URL or its code anywhere else.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-connectors')}
      alt="MCP Connectors showing connected servers, Test controls, and a catalog of services in an example configuration."
      caption="A connection has a status, a scope, and an explicit Test action"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <p>
      The full-size image only has example config too. OAuth credentials live in the adapter’s
      credential store, not in Phosphor.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>3. Test the connection, then the task</h2>
    <p>
      Choose <strong>Test</strong> on the server’s row. It opens a fresh connection through the adapter
      and reports Up with a tool count, Needs sign-in, Down, Disabled, Not in config, or Test inconclusive.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signing in and testing don’t call a model, so they cost no tokens. With no live session, a
      <strong>State unknown</strong> result doesn’t mean it’s broken. Cached tool names don’t mean the
      service is reachable either.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start a new session after changing config. Ask for something small and read-only, like “Find
      the Linear issue I specify and summarize its acceptance criteria. Do not change it.” Check the
      tool result, not just the answer. Tool calls during a task use normal model tokens and may
      count against the service’s quota.
    </p>
    <p>
      By default the gateway lets the agent look up tool details when it needs them, instead of
      sending every schema with every request. Configuring tools as direct skips that and uses more
      context.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="catalog">
    <h2>What each service needs</h2>
    <div class="connector-requirements">
      {
        connectors.map(({ id, name, use, note, docs }) => (
          <article id={id}>
            <>
              <p class="eyebrow">{use}</p>
              <h3>{name}</h3>
              <p>{note}</p>
              <a href={docs}>{name} setup docs ↗</a>
            </>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p>
      The tools you get depend on the server, your account, and the scopes you grant. Supabase and
      Questrade default to read-only; other connectors don’t. Supabase’s read-only endpoint also
      doesn’t shrink the OAuth grant, so the token still has write scopes. Pin it to a project and
      review its permissions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="custom">
    <h2>Add a custom MCP server</h2>
    <p>
      In MCP Connectors, add a custom server with either a URL or a local command and its arguments,
      and pick a scope. A command-based server runs a program on your machine, so review it like any
      package you install.
    </p>
    <p>
      Test it after saving, then start a new session to load it. If a project server overrides a
      global one with the same name, Advanced shows the raw config and which one wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using your Claude subscription? Add servers here too. Phosphor keeps the Claude CLI’s own MCP
      config and claude.ai connectors out on purpose, so they aren’t imported.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Permissions and troubleshooting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Needs sign-in:</strong> use Sign in or Reconnect for the account you want.</li><li
      >
        <strong>Down or inconclusive:</strong> check the server URL, your network, that the adapter is
        installed, and anything a local command needs. Then Test again.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Slack’s Add is disabled:</strong> finish setting up your Slack app first: client ID, redirect
        URI, PKCE, and scopes. “Set up the app” on the card has the manifest.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Tools missing from a running chat:</strong> start a new session. The adapter only reads
        its config at startup.
      </li><li>
        <strong>The wrong config wins:</strong> check scope and overrides in Advanced instead of copying
        credentials into more files.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Grant only the access the agent needs. Some tools can change data in the service. Worktrees
      and the artifact sandbox don’t limit what a connected service can do. Keep credentials and
      callback URLs out of bug reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('mcp.md')}>MCP configuration, scope resolution, and OAuth reference</a> · <a
        href="/guides/extensions/#mcp">MCP adapter package</a
      > · <a href={piDoc('mcp')}>pi’s built-in MCP</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
